WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.880
When I was a child,
I was about eight years old,

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:07.160
and I went to climb on a house
that was under construction

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.560
in our neighbourhood.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.680
So I stepped up to the edge
and I fell,

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.200
and the fall seemed
to take a very long time,

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.440
so then I was looking down
at the ground watching

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.840
the red brick floor come towards me
and once I hit the ground

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.960
then I went unconscious but it got
me interested in the question of...

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.120
When I grew up and
I became a neuroscientist,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.360
what I realised was we all come
into the world with this idea

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.200
that time is just a river that’s
flowing forward in one direction

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.880
at a fixed speed, but what we know
is that it can be different

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.480
in your head and in my head,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.800
because it's somehow
a psychological construct, time.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.040
In other words, your brain is locked
in silence and darkness

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.840
inside the vault of your skull
and its job is to figure out

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.840
what's happening outside but
it has to do a lot of editing tricks.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.680
Your vision and your hearing
process signals at different speeds

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.600
and yet, when you see
something like a balloon pop

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.440
or somebody clapping their hands,

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.520
it appears as though the sight
and sound are synchronised.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.760
And what that means is the brain has
to be collecting all the information

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.000
before it puts together a final
story and serves that up

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.720
to your conscious perception.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.400
It's like there's a buffer where
it looks for other signals

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.080
coming up the pipeline
and as a result

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.000
it means that we're all living
a little bit in the past.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.600
What we think is happening right
now has actually already transpired

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:50.120
some time ago, probably in the
ballpark of about half a second ago.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.360
In the lab if I show you a photograph
for half a second on the screen

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.920
and then I show you that same
photograph again for half a second

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.000
and then again and again and again.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.320
And now I show you
a different photograph

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.920
for the same amount of time,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.320
it will seem as though the new
photograph, the oddball,

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.120
stays on the screen
for a much longer time.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.240
Essentially when the brain
sees something that's novel,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.800
it has to burn more energy
to represent it

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.480
because it wasn't expecting that.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.640
This feeling that things are going in
slow motion is a trick of memory.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.200
In other words, when you're
in an emergency situation

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.000
a part of the brain called
the amygdala comes online,

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:32.440
this is your emergency
control centre,

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.160
it lays down memories on what
amounts to a secondary memory track,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.040
these are very dense memories.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.280
And you're noticing everything
around you and writing it all down.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.400
So when the brain
reads that back out,

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.040
there's such a density
of memory there,

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:53.680
that the brain's only conclusion is
that must have taken a long time.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.720
And I think this offers an
explanation for why people think

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.160
that time seems to speed up
as they grow older.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.080
And it's because when you’re a child,
everything's new to you.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.960
You're figuring out
the rules of the world,

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:10.720
you're writing down a lot of memory,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.080
and so when you look back
at the end of a year,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.560
you have a lot of memory
of what you've learnt.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.960
But when you're much older and
you look back at the end of the year,

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.160
you're probably doing approximately
the same stuff you've been doing

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.720
for the X number of previous years.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:32.000
And so it seems like the year
just went by in a flash.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.120
Really the way to feel as though
you've lived longer

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:39.760
is to seek novelty.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.080
So you can start
with something simple

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.400
like putting your wristwatch
on your other hand

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.320
or brushing your teeth
with your other hand.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:52.600
Something this simple just forces
the brain into a new mode

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.800
where it can't predict exactly
what's going to happen

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.400
but instead has to be engaged.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.000
And what that means is when
you go to bed at night time

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:00.960
you have a lot of footage
to draw upon

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.440
and it feels like your life
is lasting longer.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.320
Thanks for watching :)

